  "sourceNotes": {
    "raw": "The particle 呀 (aa3) softens statements and questions, making them sound friendly rather than abrupt. In 你好呀, it transforms a neutral greeting into a warm one. Cantonese uses 番 (faan1) after verbs to indicate 'doing something again' or 'back' - 確認番 means 'to confirm (again)'. This is more common than Mandarin 再.\n\nTitles like 先生 (sin1 saang1) and 小姐 (siu2 ze2) follow surnames in formal contexts. Unlike English, never use the given name with these titles. 王先生 is correct; 大明先生 sounds unnatural.\n\n喂 (wai3) serves as both 'hello' in phone conversations and as an attention-getter. The tone rises slightly when answering a call. 啲 (di1) makes nouns plural or indicates 'some' - 啲資料 means 'the information' or 'some information' depending on context.\n\nThe phrase 冇問題 (mou5 man6 tai4) literally means 'no problem' and functions like English 'sure' or 'no problem'. Avoid saying 有問題 when you mean 'okay' - it means 'there's a problem'."
  }
